Attach a `TextEditingController` to the `TextField` and read its `text` property. The controller holds the current string the user typed, which you can access at any time, for example when a button is pressed. Example:
```dart
final TextEditingController _controller = TextEditingController();
// ...
TextField(controller: _controller);
// later
String typed = _controller.text;
```

Use a ListView and set its scrollDirection to Axis.horizontal, which changes the default vertical scrolling to horizontal. This makes the list scroll sideways and you can provide children as usual. Example:
```dart
ListView(
  scrollDirection: Axis.horizontal,
  children: [
    for (final color in Colors.primaries)
      Container(width: 160, color: color),
  ],
),
```

Add the `shared_preferences` package to your project and import it. On startup obtain the instance with `await SharedPreferences.getInstance()`, read the saved integer with `prefs.getInt('counter') ?? 0`, and after each change store it using `prefs.setInt('counter', _counter)`. This writes the value to disk so it is available after the app is closed and reopened. Example:
```dart
final prefs = await SharedPreferences.getInstance();
_counter = prefs.getInt('counter') ?? 0;
await prefs.setInt('counter', _counter);
```

Create a ThemeData object that specifies the colors and TextStyle values you want for the whole app, and pass it to the `theme` property of your `MaterialApp`. This makes the defined colors and text styles available through `Theme.of(context)` for any widget in the widget tree. You can customize the color scheme, textTheme, and other style properties inside ThemeData. Example:
```dart
MaterialApp(
  theme: ThemeData(
    primaryColor: Colors.blue,
    textTheme: TextTheme(bodyText2: TextStyle(fontSize: 16, color: Colors.black)),
  ),
  home: MyHomePage(),
);
```

Offload the heavy JSON decoding to a background isolate so the UI thread stays responsive. Use `Isolate.run` to decode the JSON string and map it to your model objects, await the result, and then update the UI in `setState`. This moves the CPU‑intensive work off the main event loop and prevents the UI from freezing. Example:
```dart
final List<Photo> photos = await Isolate.run<List<Photo>>(() {
  final List<Object?> photoData = jsonDecode(jsonString) as List<Object?>;
  return photoData.cast<Map<String, Object?>>().map(Photo.fromJson).toList();
});
```
